Spec dir for downstream-wpe-2.46-rpi4.

cmdline.txt.append.disabled -- forces a display that is not physically there.
**DISABLED 2026-08-21 after it took the board off the network.** The mechanism
is described below and is still believed right; what was wrong was proving it.
See "The incident" at the end.

The rpi4 is headless: both HDMI connectors read `disconnected`, so weston's DRM
backend has no output and weston.service fails. Speedometer is gpu-class by
this repo's own definition (cmd/bench: "a rendering measurement [that] needs a
real compositor on a real GPU"), so there is nothing to run it on.

`video=HDMI-A-1:1920x1080M@60D` is the KMS way to say "drive this connector at
this mode even with no EDID" -- the trailing `D` forces digital output. It is
what a dummy HDMI plug does, in software, and the important part is *what it
does not change*: the compositor is still weston on the real vc4/v3d GPU
through the real DRM/KMS path, not a software renderer and not RDP. The legacy
`hdmi_force_hotplug=1` in config.txt does not do this under
`dtoverlay=vc4-kms-v3d`, which is why the setting is here and not there.

**A run made this way is a proof that the path works, not a comparable
number.** The mode is invented rather than negotiated, so it is not the mode a
real panel would give, and MotionMark and Speedometer both scale with the
surface being drawn (cmd/bench records `display` for exactly that reason).
Recorded as `display_forced` in the manifest so no result from it can be
mistaken for one taken against real hardware. Delete this file when a dummy
plug or a monitor is attached, and the image goes back to negotiating a mode.


## The incident, 2026-08-21

This setting was written into the image, the image was written to the stick, and
the stick was booted, in one step. The board never came back: pingable for a
minute or two while resetting ssh mid-key-exchange, then gone from the
neighbour table entirely. It needs a person to pull the stick.

**Established 2026-08-22 by removing one variable:** the same image, same
stick, same everything, with `video=HDMI-A-1:1920x1080M@60D` deleted from
cmdline.txt and nothing else changed, boots and is reachable. So this setting
is the cause, and the 2.46 image is fine.

Why it can break a boot, which was not obvious and was argued the wrong way
first: forcing a mode on a connector with nothing attached leaves vc4's KMS
driver waiting on hardware that will not answer, and a driver that hangs in
probe hangs the boot. "A display setting cannot stop userspace" is false when
the setting is handled by a driver that boot waits on.

What made it unrecoverable is worth more than the cause:

- **An armed stick that boots but hangs is sticky.** Firmware finds
  `start4.elf`, so it keeps choosing the stick; a power cycle re-enters the
  same hang rather than falling through to the SD rescue. The self-disarm lives
  in the rootfs and never runs. `docs/HANDOFF-benchmarking.md` already records
  this residual case -- this is it happening.
- **These images carry no `panic=10`.** The distro profiles set it, so a distro
  image that cannot find its root reboots; a Yocto image hangs. Adding it here
  would not have helped on its own (the reboot would re-enter the same stick),
  but the asymmetry is undocumented and worth knowing.
- **There is no console.** moose's BMC would have shown the boot; the rpi4 has
  no such path, and `docs/HANDOFF-moose-bench.md`'s "get the console working
  before the first risky boot" applies to every board, not just moose.

The order that was available and was not taken: put `video=` on the **SD**
system first, where a bad kernel argument still leaves the stick as an unarmed
fall-through, and only bake it into the stick once it has booted something.
Re-enable this file by dropping the `.disabled` suffix, and do it that way.
